1
00:00:19,447 --> 00:00:22,268
[SPEAKER_00]: I have a few more questions from around the world.

2
00:00:26,128 --> 00:00:28,309
[SPEAKER_00]: These two are related.

3
00:00:32,029 --> 00:00:37,910
[SPEAKER_00]: They are in terms of becoming a Sotāpanna, really.

4
00:00:39,310 --> 00:00:47,732
[SPEAKER_00]: So, one friend asks, due to an insight that I had a year ago,

5
00:00:52,129 --> 00:00:53,771
[SPEAKER_00]: I take things less personally.

6
00:00:54,832 --> 00:00:57,034
[SPEAKER_00]: And my aversion is really minimum.

7
00:00:57,995 --> 00:01:00,657
[SPEAKER_00]: And when it happens, it doesn't last long at all.

8
00:01:01,978 --> 00:01:05,262
[SPEAKER_00]: But I can see that I am still liable to aversion.

9
00:01:06,551 --> 00:01:18,896
[SPEAKER_00]: So my practice right now is holding on to that notion of non-self that I saw, and the non-ownership as the general background tone of life, or the larger container of things.

10
00:01:19,436 --> 00:01:20,697
[SPEAKER_00]: How should I proceed?

11
00:01:22,858 --> 00:01:30,861
[SPEAKER_00]: And this person is actually just a lay person who's living at home, very kind of relaxed lifestyle.

12
00:01:31,702 --> 00:01:31,922
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

13
00:01:32,562 --> 00:01:34,923
[SPEAKER_00]: But beyond that, I don't know what they do.

14
00:01:37,009 --> 00:01:39,251
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, how should I proceed?

15
00:01:40,692 --> 00:01:45,615
[SPEAKER_01]: It's really more of a... Is there anything else I should do?

16
00:01:46,256 --> 00:01:46,916
[SPEAKER_01]: type of thing.

17
00:01:48,197 --> 00:01:54,301
[SPEAKER_01]: Because what's described is fine, but the question is, why are you asking yourself, how should I proceed?

18
00:01:55,122 --> 00:01:56,602
[SPEAKER_01]: Is what you describe is not enough?

19
00:01:58,204 --> 00:02:00,085
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you aware there is more to be done?

20
00:02:01,866 --> 00:02:03,147
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you not sure about it?

21
00:02:05,978 --> 00:02:24,390
[SPEAKER_01]: If all these questions persist, means you can't say you are beyond doubt, which means you should proceed by doing more of the same and then investigate in what areas you can improve and you might have been lacking.

22
00:02:25,471 --> 00:02:33,717
[SPEAKER_01]: So, maybe you had an insight that freed you from the sense of ownership to a degree, which is possible, you know, even people who don't

23
00:02:34,947 --> 00:02:37,529
[SPEAKER_01]: We don't necessarily commit to the practice 24-7.

24
00:02:37,689 --> 00:02:42,673
[SPEAKER_01]: They could still experience non-ownership and experience release on account of it.

25
00:02:43,434 --> 00:02:52,962
[SPEAKER_01]: But is there a cultivation of a suitable basis for that insight to be taken then even further?

26
00:02:53,843 --> 00:02:55,604
[SPEAKER_01]: Such as, is there a sufficient virtue?

27
00:02:56,305 --> 00:02:59,427
[SPEAKER_01]: Accomplishment in virtue, sense restraint, seeing the signs of your mind.

28
00:02:59,787 --> 00:03:02,790
[SPEAKER_01]: All those prerequisites, basically, for a Sotāpanna.

29
00:03:04,507 --> 00:03:11,592
[SPEAKER_01]: People don't have the right view, not because, you know, dhamma is some secret mystery that they still haven't figured out.

30
00:03:12,953 --> 00:03:17,777
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because the things that they read and the things that they understand do not pertain deeply enough.

31
00:03:18,057 --> 00:03:23,220
[SPEAKER_01]: And they don't pertain deeply enough not because of the lack of information or lack of their intelligence to understand.

32
00:03:23,621 --> 00:03:33,668
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because of the lack of the sufficient basis of that morality, virtue, accomplishment, solitude, sign of the mind, guarding of the sense doors.

33
00:03:35,106 --> 00:03:41,679
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if that basis is thoroughly developed, very little instruction will be necessary to understand the not-self.

34
00:03:42,760 --> 00:03:44,061
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, cultivate it further.

35
00:03:44,662 --> 00:03:48,105
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like that idea of not-self.

36
00:03:48,406 --> 00:03:55,332
[SPEAKER_00]: A person can get that idea, that theory, but it remains a theory until you actually live in accordance with that idea.

37
00:03:55,352 --> 00:04:03,180
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and even as a theory, as I said, it can have a, you know, it can be theoretical to some degree, but it has its practical side of it.

38
00:04:03,861 --> 00:04:06,223
[SPEAKER_01]: But that practicality is something you don't want to...

39
00:04:07,273 --> 00:04:11,797
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't want to be sort of living off of it, so to speak.

40
00:04:12,257 --> 00:04:16,901
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you had some like a practical release, fine, now have thousand more of those.

41
00:04:18,862 --> 00:04:19,863
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not about having one.

42
00:04:19,883 --> 00:04:36,517
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, the Buddha said to even the actual sotāpānas, actual sakadāgāmīs, actual anāgāmīs, he said, did you understand the dhamma for the sake of cherishing that view that you have, or for the sake of abandoning even that through your repetitive work and diligence until you have crossed the shore?

43
00:04:37,637 --> 00:04:38,838
[SPEAKER_01]: And it says, no, it's for understanding.

44
00:04:38,878 --> 00:04:40,519
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, keep digging.

45
00:04:40,579 --> 00:04:42,861
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't stop, because you're anāgāmī now, or something.

46
00:04:44,182 --> 00:04:47,184
[SPEAKER_01]: Keep digging, and don't carry that raft with you when you cross the shore.

47
00:04:47,204 --> 00:04:48,045
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, that's it.

48
00:04:48,165 --> 00:04:49,146
[SPEAKER_00]: It's for that purpose.

49
00:04:50,167 --> 00:04:51,367
[SPEAKER_00]: Continue that renunciation.

50
00:04:51,427 --> 00:04:51,868
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

51
00:04:51,928 --> 00:04:56,651
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you have it, if you've had any kind of insight of any kind, sure.

52
00:04:57,012 --> 00:04:59,874
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, a thousand times more, repeat the same.

53
00:05:02,336 --> 00:05:04,818
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, when you do, just keep doing it.

54
00:05:06,153 --> 00:05:06,993
[SPEAKER_01]: And keep increasing it.

55
00:05:07,073 --> 00:05:08,514
[SPEAKER_01]: And you'll know when to stop.

56
00:05:09,494 --> 00:05:09,734
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

57
00:05:11,135 --> 00:05:17,757
[SPEAKER_00]: It's like that you get the the right idea, but then you need to... it needs to root itself.

58
00:05:18,057 --> 00:05:18,237
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

59
00:05:18,437 --> 00:05:22,498
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, you bring it into reality, you bring it into realization.

60
00:05:23,051 --> 00:05:24,331
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to cultivate it.

61
00:05:24,631 --> 00:05:35,653
[SPEAKER_01]: You might plant the best seeds, but unless you look after and clear the weeds and then protect it from becoming too dry or too wet, they're not gonna germinate properly or they will not be as good.

62
00:05:35,773 --> 00:05:37,134
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have to attend to it.

63
00:05:37,594 --> 00:05:39,414
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to keep doing it and keep an eye on it.

64
00:05:41,274 --> 00:05:42,675
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what do I do next?

65
00:05:42,735 --> 00:05:44,795
[SPEAKER_01]: It's more like, how do I do more of this?

66
00:05:46,195 --> 00:05:48,736
[SPEAKER_01]: Even increase the quality of what I've been doing.

67
00:05:51,981 --> 00:05:54,765
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's... how to do more of that?

68
00:05:54,926 --> 00:05:56,809
[SPEAKER_00]: Those instructions are already there.

69
00:05:56,929 --> 00:05:57,510
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, exactly.

70
00:05:57,550 --> 00:05:58,652
[SPEAKER_01]: How do I do more of this?

71
00:05:58,732 --> 00:06:00,295
[SPEAKER_01]: Means I already know how I've done this.

72
00:06:00,816 --> 00:06:01,978
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now just do more of it.

73
00:06:03,112 --> 00:06:03,932
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a secret.

74
00:06:03,992 --> 00:06:07,934
[SPEAKER_01]: It's more like on the level of diligence, work, repetition and sticking to it.

75
00:06:08,735 --> 00:06:12,436
[SPEAKER_01]: And yes, not living off on the that kind of...

76
00:06:12,456 --> 00:06:13,757
[SPEAKER_01]: I had this experience a year ago.

77
00:06:14,477 --> 00:06:16,638
[SPEAKER_01]: It was part... Nobody denies that people have experiences.

78
00:06:17,359 --> 00:06:26,083
[SPEAKER_01]: What you deny, what I deny, is basically the view that you can just now assume it's finished or something because you saw it once.

79
00:06:28,032 --> 00:06:29,294
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but I had experience it was real.

80
00:06:29,334 --> 00:06:29,755
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, sure.

81
00:06:30,636 --> 00:06:33,960
[SPEAKER_01]: But you also had experience of suffering millions of times and it was equally real.

82
00:06:34,741 --> 00:06:35,543
[SPEAKER_01]: So, which is more now?

83
00:06:36,304 --> 00:06:40,109
[SPEAKER_01]: Your one experience of release or every single time you still suffer?

84
00:06:41,751 --> 00:06:42,252
[SPEAKER_01]: So, there you go.

85
00:06:42,372 --> 00:06:42,953
[SPEAKER_01]: That's your answer.

86
00:06:43,253 --> 00:06:43,994
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you still suffer?

87
00:06:44,154 --> 00:06:44,335
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

88
00:06:46,337 --> 00:06:50,741
[SPEAKER_01]: Keep then regenerating, cultivating that experience in ourself.

89
00:06:51,102 --> 00:06:52,383
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's not clear, clarify it.

90
00:06:52,483 --> 00:06:53,044
[SPEAKER_01]: That's your work.

91
00:06:53,784 --> 00:06:56,006
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's clear, then sustain it in its clarity.

92
00:06:56,487 --> 00:06:57,047
[SPEAKER_01]: Protect it.

93
00:06:57,888 --> 00:07:03,033
[SPEAKER_01]: And make sure the virtue is there to match, to sustain that basis.

94
00:07:03,113 --> 00:07:03,874
[SPEAKER_01]: Sense restraint.

95
00:07:04,034 --> 00:07:05,856
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't become careless and keep doing it.

96
00:07:08,350 --> 00:07:10,752
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, you keep clarifying, polishing that view.

97
00:07:11,332 --> 00:07:12,073
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not like that.

98
00:07:12,453 --> 00:07:17,897
[SPEAKER_00]: Realization, realization, bringing it, you're bringing it into reality, so to speak.

99
00:07:17,917 --> 00:07:18,777
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah, sure, sure, sure.

100
00:07:18,797 --> 00:07:19,258
[SPEAKER_01]: Making it more real.

101
00:07:19,338 --> 00:07:20,819
[SPEAKER_01]: Literal meaning, yeah, exactly.

102
00:07:21,059 --> 00:07:24,061
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like an insight, but then you just... Well, that's what bhāvanā is.

103
00:07:24,722 --> 00:07:30,586
[SPEAKER_01]: Keeping it alive, bhāvanā, keeping it in the being, bringing it into the actuality, the being of it.

104
00:07:31,847 --> 00:07:35,129
[SPEAKER_01]: Sustaining it in its existence, whatever you are cultivating.

105
00:07:35,889 --> 00:07:36,530
[SPEAKER_00]: Until it takes...

106
00:07:37,991 --> 00:07:39,492
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you'll know, as I said, when to stop.

107
00:07:39,572 --> 00:07:40,993
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't stop by decision to stop.

108
00:07:41,954 --> 00:07:43,495
[SPEAKER_01]: You stop when there's no more work to be done.

109
00:07:44,616 --> 00:07:45,737
[SPEAKER_01]: And you don't need to decide that.

110
00:07:45,957 --> 00:07:46,618
[SPEAKER_01]: That just happens.

111
00:07:54,624 --> 00:08:00,268
[SPEAKER_00]: So, the other question from another friend, he or she says,

112
00:08:01,929 --> 00:08:11,095
[SPEAKER_00]: I've reached a point where I can clearly see arising feelings and thoughts as not mine and simply just as a risen phenomenon.

113
00:08:12,396 --> 00:08:14,378
[SPEAKER_00]: But sometimes I lapse in mindfulness.

114
00:08:14,818 --> 00:08:18,741
[SPEAKER_00]: I lose the correct perspective and suffer as a result.

115
00:08:21,042 --> 00:08:25,866
[SPEAKER_00]: Also, I think every time a feeling comes up, there is a self bundled with it.

116
00:08:26,106 --> 00:08:30,349
[SPEAKER_00]: It takes about a second for me to realize it's anicca, dukkha, anatta.

117
00:08:30,827 --> 00:08:31,348
[SPEAKER_01]: That's too long.

118
00:08:32,969 --> 00:08:38,556
[SPEAKER_00]: So, I'm wondering, if I've reached the Sotāpanna stage, if I haven't, how far do I have to go?

119
00:08:38,596 --> 00:08:38,816
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

120
00:08:39,797 --> 00:08:43,281
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, if you're wondering whether you have reached it, you haven't.

121
00:08:45,223 --> 00:08:48,948
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, if you need somebody else to answer it, means you don't know it for yourself.

122
00:08:50,495 --> 00:08:52,837
[SPEAKER_00]: Like you just said, once you finish the work, you know.

123
00:08:54,039 --> 00:08:54,499
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

124
00:08:54,579 --> 00:08:58,483
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not, it's not gonna, it's not like, oh, I can stop now because I'm a sotāpanna.

125
00:08:58,503 --> 00:09:05,790
[SPEAKER_01]: It's more like, no, it's because I'm a sotāpanna, I just, the work cannot happen anymore, because there's no more work, to that extent.

126
00:09:06,111 --> 00:09:06,331
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

127
00:09:06,952 --> 00:09:11,977
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, you keep looking for work, and there is no more work, and then you calm down, and then you realize there is no more work.

128
00:09:13,166 --> 00:09:14,847
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how suttas always describe it as well.

129
00:09:15,368 --> 00:09:16,108
[SPEAKER_01]: He works diligently.

130
00:09:16,128 --> 00:09:18,050
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like, oh, is this handle worn out yet?

131
00:09:18,070 --> 00:09:18,190
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

132
00:09:18,210 --> 00:09:19,070
[SPEAKER_01]: He just uses the handle.

133
00:09:19,090 --> 00:09:22,013
[SPEAKER_01]: He uses it until he realizes it's worn out and then it needs to be replaced.

134
00:09:22,533 --> 00:09:26,936
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's saying that a monk just purifies his mind, purifies his mind, purifies his mind.

135
00:09:27,237 --> 00:09:28,337
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't go, oh, when will I?

136
00:09:28,357 --> 00:09:29,278
[SPEAKER_01]: When will I not?

137
00:09:29,338 --> 00:09:29,638
[SPEAKER_01]: Will I?

138
00:09:29,698 --> 00:09:29,999
[SPEAKER_01]: Am I?

139
00:09:30,319 --> 00:09:32,100
[SPEAKER_01]: He just keeps doing it, keeps doing it, keeps doing it.

140
00:09:32,481 --> 00:09:36,003
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, when there's no more work to be done, he just obviously has to drop it and calm down.

141
00:09:36,123 --> 00:09:36,403
[SPEAKER_01]: And then,

142
00:09:45,070 --> 00:09:50,274
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you're asking if you're a sotāpanna, it means you're not.

143
00:09:50,614 --> 00:09:51,995
[SPEAKER_01]: That doesn't mean what you're doing is wrong.

144
00:09:52,816 --> 00:09:59,421
[SPEAKER_01]: It just means you have to keep doing it more until basically you are unable to suffer amidst things that are making you suffer.

145
00:10:00,902 --> 00:10:04,845
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, whether the whole world says you're a sotāpanna or you're not a sotāpanna, it doesn't matter.

146
00:10:04,945 --> 00:10:06,687
[SPEAKER_01]: You went beyond suffering to that extent.

147
00:10:07,647 --> 00:10:09,409
[SPEAKER_01]: And the reason why

148
00:10:11,517 --> 00:10:19,839
[SPEAKER_01]: That person cannot sustain mindfulness, or rather mindfulness lapse, and then they suffer, because they lose perspective.

149
00:10:20,519 --> 00:10:22,260
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because the mind hasn't been developed enough.

150
00:10:23,320 --> 00:10:27,401
[SPEAKER_01]: And you don't develop mind enough by getting more information, that can help.

151
00:10:27,681 --> 00:10:30,982
[SPEAKER_01]: You develop the mind by sense restraint and upgrading the virtue.

152
00:10:32,934 --> 00:10:33,814
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the work.

153
00:10:34,135 --> 00:10:47,421
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why Buddha said, you know, if a monk keeps patimokkha, dwells restrained, moderate in eating, guards the sense doors, the mind becomes pliable, as a result of that training and taming.

154
00:10:48,302 --> 00:10:53,565
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what he thinks, then the mind will go where he thinks it, and he will be in control of it, not the other way around.

155
00:10:54,145 --> 00:10:56,966
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that person, obviously, doesn't want to be losing the perspective.

156
00:10:57,427 --> 00:11:00,108
[SPEAKER_01]: Because when they do, they're aware suffering arises and persists.

157
00:11:02,120 --> 00:11:06,385
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, if it were up to them, the mind would not lose the perspective.

158
00:11:06,986 --> 00:11:08,188
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the mind doesn't listen to them.

159
00:11:08,648 --> 00:11:09,689
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind loses perspective.

160
00:11:10,430 --> 00:11:11,652
[SPEAKER_01]: Why can the mind do that?

161
00:11:11,992 --> 00:11:18,300
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because you still give in to the choices that enable mind to have control over you, not other way around.

162
00:11:18,680 --> 00:11:19,662
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the mind is not tamed.

163
00:11:21,379 --> 00:11:31,444
[SPEAKER_01]: So, whenever you can't sustain what you understood, or you're lapsing, or you're getting distracted, it's because you still keep choosing things rooted in greed, aversion and delusion.

164
00:11:32,164 --> 00:11:34,246
[SPEAKER_01]: And you're still acting out of distraction.

165
00:11:36,387 --> 00:11:38,608
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you need to upgrade the precepts.

166
00:11:40,042 --> 00:11:44,166
[SPEAKER_01]: Or take them a bit more seriously or tightly and also guard the sense doors.

167
00:11:45,086 --> 00:11:53,294
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, eh... if you can't do that, then you have to accept the outcome, then the mind will get out of your control every now and then and you will suffer.

168
00:11:54,014 --> 00:11:58,858
[SPEAKER_01]: And that suffering will be worse than somebody who is not aware of the right context.

169
00:11:59,959 --> 00:12:01,501
[SPEAKER_01]: Because ignorance is bliss to a degree.

170
00:12:02,281 --> 00:12:07,086
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why people do not see the suffering in sensuality and remain infatuated for the rest of their life.

171
00:12:08,210 --> 00:12:09,671
[SPEAKER_01]: They pay the price later.

172
00:12:09,711 --> 00:12:10,592
[SPEAKER_01]: Much worse price.

173
00:12:11,332 --> 00:12:19,798
[SPEAKER_01]: But somebody who is aware and who loses perspective, that suffering will be felt worse, because they are aware of it.

174
00:12:19,858 --> 00:12:25,982
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, the compromise of not being sufficiently restrained, also then has more weight.

175
00:12:26,182 --> 00:12:28,644
[SPEAKER_01]: So you realize, yeah, it is up to you.

176
00:12:28,824 --> 00:12:29,725
[SPEAKER_01]: And there's no excuse.

177
00:12:30,345 --> 00:12:33,047
[SPEAKER_01]: So if your mind is not in your control, it's because you haven't tamed it enough.

